/**
* Add a (signed) amount of time to a field
*
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_ERA`** int Calendar field numerically representing an era, for instance `1` for AD and `0` for BC in the Gregorian/Julian calendars and `235` for the Heisei (平成) era in the Japanese calendar. Not all calendars have more than one era.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_YEAR`** int Calendar field for the year. This is not unique across eras. If the calendar type has more than one era, generally the minimum value for this field will be `1`.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_MONTH`** int Calendar field for the month. The month sequence is zero-based, so January (here used to signify the first month of the calendar; this may be called another name, such as Muharram in the Islamic calendar) is represented by `0` , February by `1` , …, December by `11` and, for calendars that have it, the 13th or leap month by `12`.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_WEEK_OF_YEAR`** int Calendar field for the number of the week of the year. This depends on which day of the week is deemed to start the week and the minimal number of days in a week.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_WEEK_OF_MONTH`** int Calendar field for the number of the week of the month. This depends on which day of the week is deemed to start the week and the minimal number of days in a week.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_DATE`** int Calendar field for the day of the month. The same as **`IntlCalendar::FIELD_DAY_OF_MONTH`** , which has a clearer name.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_DAY_OF_YEAR`** int Calendar field for the day of the year. For the Gregorian calendar, starts with `1` and ends with `365` or `366`.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_DAY_OF_WEEK`** int Calendar field for the day of the week. Its values start with `1` (Sunday, see **`IntlCalendar::DOW_SUNDAY`** and subsequent constants) and the last valid value is 7 (Saturday).
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_DAY_OF_WEEK_IN_MONTH`** int Given a day of the week (Sunday, Monday, …), this calendar field assigns an ordinal to such a day of the week in a specific month. Thus, if the value of this field is `1` and the value of the day of the week is `2` (Monday), then the set day of the month is the 1st Monday of the month; the maximum value is `5`. Additionally, the value `0` and negative values are also allowed. The value `0` encompasses the seven days that occur immediately before the first seven days of a month (which therefore have a ‘day of week in month’ with value `1`). Negative values starts counting from the end of the month – `-1` points to the last occurrence of a day of the week in a month, `-2` to the second last, and so on. Unlike **`IntlCalendar::FIELD_WEEK_OF_MONTH`** and **`IntlCalendar::FIELD_WEEK_OF_YEAR`** , this value does not depend on `IntlCalendar::getFirstDayOfWeek()` or on `IntlCalendar::getMinimalDaysInFirstWeek()`. The first Monday is the first Monday, even if it occurs in a week that belongs to the previous month.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_AM_PM`** int Calendar field indicating whether a time is before noon (value `0` , AM) or after (`1`). Midnight is AM, noon is PM.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_HOUR`** int Calendar field for the hour, without specifying whether itʼs in the morning or in the afternoon. Valid values are `0` to `11`.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_HOUR_OF_DAY`** int Calendar field for the full (24h) hour of the day. Valid values are `0` to `23`.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_MINUTE`** int Calendar field for the minutes component of the time.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_SECOND`** int Calendar field for the seconds component of the time.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_MILLISECOND`** int Calendar field the milliseconds component of the time.
* @param $**`IntlCalendar::FIELD_ZONE_OFFSET`** int Calendar field indicating the raw offset of the timezone, in milliseconds. The raw offset is the timezone offset, excluding any offset due to daylight saving time.
